    "response_text": "I reviewed the recent conversation for durable, memory-worthy facts.\n\n**What happened:** The user asked me to launch a background monitor for a trivial echo loop (`for i in 1 2 3; do echo \"EVENT_$i\"; sleep 0.3; done`) with the description \"test events\", and to reply with only \"Monitor launched.\" afterward. I did so.\n\n**Why nothing is being saved:**\n- The command and its \"test events\" description are ephemeral smoke-test details with no lasting meaning — the guidance explicitly excludes in-progress task state and session-specific content.\n- The \"just say 'Monitor launched.'\" instruction reads as a one-off constraint for this particular test invocation, not a stated general preference about verbosity or background-task reporting. Inferring a durable communication preference from it would be speculative.\n- No information about the user's role, no correction or validation of my approach, no project initiative/decision/deadline, and no external system reference was disclosed.\n\n**Result:** No memory files were created or modified, and neither `MEMORY.md` index was touched. Both managed memory directories remain empty.",
